# Project Ironclad

A hands-on Linux hardening lab. I took an Ubuntu machine (WSL2), measured its
security, locked it down layer by layer, attacked it to prove each control
works, and wrote a script that checks everything automatically.

## Results

| Measure | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Lynis hardening index | 68 | 81 |
| verify.sh checks passing | n/a | 15 of 15 |
| SSH | passwords, root allowed, port 22 | keys only, no root, one user, port 2222 |
| Firewall | default | deny incoming by default, only needed ports open |

#Environment
Ubuntu on WSL2 (Windows). Tools: OpenSSH, ufw, fail2ban,
inotify-tools, Lynis. All tests were run from Windows PowerShell as the
"attacker" against the WSL machine.

## What I did

# Baseline
Recorded listening ports, enabled services, users and SUID files, and ran a
Lynis audit (score 68). 


# SSH hardening
Key-only login, no root, AllowUsers, port 2222, MaxAuthTries 3, LogLevel
VERBOSE. Tested: password login refused, root refused, old port closed.


# Firewall (ufw)
Deny incoming by default, rate-limited SSH, only the lab web port open.

# fail2ban
Three failed logins ban an address for ten minutes. 

# Change detection
watch.sh logs changes to passwd, shadow, sudoers and the SSH config.


# System hardening
sysctl network and kernel settings, blocked unused protocols, reviewed
services and ports, automatic security updates.

# Verification
verify.sh prints PASS or FAIL for 15 controls. A second Lynis audit
raised the score from 68 to 81.

## How to run the verification
    ./verify.sh
Expected: "15 passed, 0 failed".

# Attack drills
| Drill | Result |
|---|---|
| Typo in SSH config | sshd -t caught it before restart |
| Password guessing | fail2ban banned the address |
| Unauthorized change | watch.sh logged the edit |
| Firewall disabled | verify.sh reported FAIL |
| Rollback | restored from backup |

## How this maps to AWS
| Linux control | AWS equivalent |
|---|---|
| ufw | Security groups, network ACLs |
| SSH keys, AllowUsers | IAM, SSM Session Manager |
| fail2ban | GuardDuty, WAF rate-based rules |
| auditd / watch.sh | CloudTrail |
| unattended-upgrades | Systems Manager Patch Manager |
| Lynis | Security Hub, Inspector, CIS benchmarks |


## What I would do on a real cloud server
HTTPS everywhere, central log shipping (CloudWatch Logs), SSM Session Manager
instead of SSH, patching with Patch Manager, and the whole setup as code
(Terraform).
